.TH CCOL_SCOPED_PTR 3 "2026-07-18" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_scoped_ptr \- declare a raw pointer that is freed automatically at scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/common.h>

ccol_scoped_ptr(name, type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares "type *name" and sets it to NULL. It also registers an
automatic cleanup with __attribute__((cleanup(...))). Assign to name in
the normal way, for example with a plain malloc(). The macro frees the
value that name holds at the end of the enclosing scope. It frees that
value with the default allocator, which is ccol_mem_free(), a plain
free().

This is the same as ccol_scoped_ptr_mp(name, type, NULL). Use
ccol_scoped_ptr_mp() when a custom allocator for the whole library must
free the pointer, and not the default allocator.

The other containers in this library give you a *_construct_scoped
macro. This macro is different, because no container type is attached to
it. It exists for a plain block on the heap that is not a chmap, a
cbmap, a cvec, or another container.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  The name of the pointer variable to declare
type  The type that the pointer points to (for example char, struct foo)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Needs GCC or Clang, because it uses __attribute__((cleanup))

The macro expands to two declarations, and not to one expression. Use it
as its own statement. Every *_construct_scoped macro in this library
already has the same constraint.

Do not lose an earlier value when you give name a new value inside the
scope. The macro frees only the last value. You must free an earlier
value yourself before you assign the new one. Every other guard that
uses the cleanup attribute works in the same way.

Use ccol_scoped_ptr_release() to give the ownership of the pointer to
code outside the enclosing scope, for example as the return value of a
function. The macro then does not free the pointer at the end of the
scope.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
The pointer has no synchronisation. It is a plain local variable. It
obeys the ordinary C rules for scope and for thread-local storage.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void process(void) {
  ccol_scoped_ptr(buf, char);
  buf = malloc(128);
  if (!buf) return;

  /* buf is used here */

}  /* buf is freed automatically here, on every return path */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr_mp (3),
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr_release (3)
